==Old English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|gem-pro|ang}} {{recons|tīmô|lang=gem-pro}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} {{recons|tī-|lang=ine-pro}}, from {{recons|dī-||time|lang=ine-pro}}. Cognate with Old Norse {{term|tími|lang=non}} (Danish {{term|time|lang=da}}, Swedish {{term|timme|lang=sv}}). Related to Old English {{term|tid|tīd|lang=ang}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈtiːma/|lang=ang}}

===Noun===
{{ang-noun|head=tīma|g=m|pl=timan|plhead=tīman}}

# A finite period of [[time]].
# A period of history, an [[era]].
# A specific period of existence or action, especially a person’s [[lifetime]] or a woman’s [[pregnancy]].
# A point in time, a recognisable part of the year, season or day.
# The proper or appropriate time for something, especially a [[birth]] or [[death]].

====Declension====
{{ang-decl-noun-n-m|tim|alt1=tīm}}

